  { id:'opmcq-c3-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"Following ocular trauma, a patient presented with enophthalmos and anesthesia of lower eyelid, cheek, side of nose, upper lip, upper teeth and gums. Which of the following is the nerve that is most probably involved in the trauma and caused these manifestations?",
    options:["Supraorbital nerve","Infraorbital nerve","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem is not a puzzle — it is a dermatome read out loud, and the territory it names belongs to the infraorbital nerve.** The book prints the list word for word as a sign of blow-out fracture: \"**Infraorbital nerve anaesthesia:** Involving the lower lid, cheek, side of the nose, upper lip, upper teeth and gums if the fracture involves the infraorbital canal\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59). `L25) Trauma I` slide 22 prints the identical sentence.\n\nThe second word of the stem confirms it. **Enophthalmos** is the book's third cause after orbital fat atrophy and cicatrising metastasis, and it is the late sign of a **blow-out fracture**: \"Patients suffer from early proptosis due to oedema, emphysema, and haematoma but later can develop **enophthalmos**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58). So the stem gives a fracture and a sensory loss, and the anatomy joins them: the fracture \"most frequently involves **the floor of the orbit** along the thin bone covering the **infraorbital canal**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58). The nerve runs in the roof of the maxillary sinus, which is the floor of the orbit; the floor cannot break without the nerve being at risk.\n\n| Option | Territory | Why it is not this |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Supraorbital** | Forehead, upper lid, scalp to the vertex | Wrong direction entirely — it is the V1 branch leaving through the supraorbital notch (`L10) orbit` slide 2), and it supplies everything the stem does *not* mention |\n| **Infraorbital** | Lower lid, cheek, side of nose, upper lip, upper teeth and gums | The stem's exact list |\n| **Facial** | Motor to the muscles of facial expression | Its injury gives weakness and lagophthalmos, not numbness. It carries no sensation to this skin |\n| **Glossopharyngeal** | Posterior third of the tongue, pharynx | Nowhere near the orbit or the midface |\n\n**A**, the supraorbital nerve, is the near-miss only by name. Both are terminal cutaneous branches of the trigeminal nerve, but of different divisions — supraorbital from the ophthalmic (V1), infraorbital from the maxillary (V2) — and they divide the face at the palpebral fissure. Numbness of the **upper teeth and gums** settles it beyond argument: no V1 branch reaches a tooth.\n\n**C**, the facial nerve, is the option that catches a reader who has stopped reading at \"cheek\". It is the only motor option here, and a motor nerve cannot produce anaesthesia. *(That the facial nerve is purely motor to the face is standard anatomy and is not taken from the course material; the ophthalmology decks name the facial nerve only in the context of lid closure and lagophthalmos.)*\n\n**D**, the glossopharyngeal nerve, is a free option — it is not an orbital or midfacial nerve in any sense, and no orbital trauma reaches it.\n\nA practical footnote the book makes explicit: the sensory loss is not itself an indication for surgery. Repair is reserved for \"muscle entrapment causing diplopia\" or \"large fractures causing marked enophthalmos\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59), and infraorbital anaesthesia often recovers slowly on its own.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Numbness of the lower lid, cheek, side of nose, upper lip, upper teeth and gums after orbital trauma is infraorbital (V2) anaesthesia, and it points to a blow-out fracture of the orbital floor through the infraorbital canal.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.23 (Ch.3 Q1)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"A 22-year-old girl was injured severely in a car accident. Initial examination revealed that she had high fever, lid edema and conjunctival injection. Ocular motility was reduced markedly. Also, RAPD was observed. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Preseptal cellulitis","Traumatic enophthalmos","Orbital cellulitis","Lagophthalmos"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Fever plus reduced ocular motility plus a relative afferent pupillary defect is orbital cellulitis, and the last two findings are what put the infection BEHIND the orbital septum.** The book's comparison table decides this question row by row (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.61):\n\n| Clinical feature | Preseptal | Orbital |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fever | May be present | **Present** |\n| Conjunctival injection | Absent | **Present** |\n| Ocular mobility | Normal | **Decreased** |\n| RAPD | Absent | **In severe cases (poor prognostic sign)** |\n| Site | Anterior to the septum | Posterior to the septum |\n\nEvery one of the stem's four findings sits in the right-hand column, and two of them — decreased motility and RAPD — are printed as **absent** on the left. `L10) orbit` slide 24 states the same rule as an exclusion: in preseptal cellulitis, \"in contrast to orbital cellulitis, proptosis and chemosis are ABSENT and VA, pupillary reactions and ocular motility are UNIMPAIRED.\"\n\n**A, preseptal cellulitis**, is therefore excluded twice over, and the exclusion is the whole point of the question. The septum is the anatomical line: infection in front of it cannot reach the extraocular muscles or the optic nerve, so it cannot restrict movement and cannot produce an afferent pupillary defect. A preseptal patient has a hot swollen lid and a normal eye underneath.\n\n**B, traumatic enophthalmos**, is the option the car accident is there to make attractive. It fails on the systemic sign: enophthalmos is a mechanical consequence of a floor fracture with fat prolapse (`L10) orbit` slide 5; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.58) and **produces no fever**. A sunken globe also does not cause conjunctival injection or an RAPD.\n\n**D, lagophthalmos**, is failure of lid closure. It is a lid sign, not a diagnosis of an inflamed orbit, and it explains none of the four findings. *(The corpus's `opqb-t3-115` lists its causes — seventh nerve palsy, thyrotoxic exophthalmos, symblepharon — none of which is a febrile post-traumatic illness.)*\n\n**The trauma is the aetiology, not the distractor.** The book gives orbital cellulitis as \"**Usually** secondary to sinus/ facial/ tooth infections **or trauma**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60), so a severe facial injury is a recognised route — a breached orbit inoculated at the roadside. The teaching version of orbital cellulitis is the febrile child with ethmoiditis (`L10) orbit` slide 26), and this stem deliberately uses the other route.\n\n**Why the RAPD is the finding that must not be shrugged off.** The book prints it as a **poor prognostic sign**, and it means the optic nerve is being compressed or inflamed inside a closed bony box. That converts the case from an infection into an emergency: \"It is an OCULAR as well as a MEDICAL EMERGENCY\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60), and the complication list runs 7–11 % significant loss of vision, cavernous sinus thrombosis, meningitis, brain abscess and death, with a mortality of 17–20 % without antibiotics. Management is admission, blood cultures, orbital CT, intravenous ceftriaxone plus vancomycin with oral metronidazole for a week, and surgical drainage of an abscess (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Fever with reduced ocular motility and an RAPD places cellulitis posterior to the orbital septum — orbital, not preseptal — and the RAPD is a poor prognostic sign demanding admission and IV antibiotics.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.23 (Ch.3 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"A 38-year-old male patient presented with unilateral pulsating exophthalmos following head trauma. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Grave's disease","Retrobulbar hemorrhage","Carotid-cavernous fistula","Cavernous sinus thrombosis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Pulsation is the word that decides this, and the book attaches it to one entity by name: \"Carotid-Cavernous fistula: (Pulsating exophthalmos)\"** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58, aetiology item 6 of exophthalmos). Head trauma supplies the second half of the diagnosis — `L10) orbit` slide 33 records that direct fistulae are high-flow shunts in which carotid blood passes into the cavernous sinus through a defect in the intracavernous internal carotid \"as a result of **TRAUMA (75%)**\".\n\n`L10) orbit` slide 34 then prints the presentation this stem is drawn from: \"Presentation may be **days or weeks after head injury** with a CLASSIC TRIAD of **PULSATILE PROPTOSIS, CONJUNCTIVAL CHEMOSIS and a WHOOSHING NOISE IN THE HEAD**.\" The mechanism is arterial pressure delivered into a venous sinus that drains the orbit: the superior ophthalmic vein arterialises, the orbit engorges, and the globe transmits the arterial pulse.\n\n**Why a pulse means an arteriovenous communication and not a mass.** `L10) orbit` slide 12 gives the general rule for orbital signs: \"**Pulsation is caused either by an arteriovenous communication or a defect in the orbital roof.**\" A tumour, a haemorrhage or an inflamed orbit pushes the globe forward steadily; only a shunt (or a missing bony roof transmitting the cerebral pulse) makes it beat. The same slide adds the bedside confirmation — a bruit \"is best heard with the bell of the stethoscope and is lessened or abolished by gently compressing the ipsilateral carotid artery in the neck\".\n\n**B, retrobulbar haemorrhage**, is the trap, because it also follows trauma and also proptoses the eye. It is separated by the tempo and by the pulse. The book calls it \"**Acute onset**\" with a \"History of trauma\" and \"Ophthalmic emergency due to markedly raised IOP with risk of CRAO\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58) — minutes to hours, not days to weeks, and it does not pulsate. This is the answer to **Q9 of this chapter**, which prints this same stem with *progressively-expanding* in place of *pulsating*.\n\n**A, Graves' disease**, is the commonest cause of proptosis in adults overall (`L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases`; and Q20 of this chapter) — but it is autoimmune, has no relation to head trauma, is typically bilateral or bilateral-asymmetrical, and never pulsates.\n\n**D, cavernous sinus thrombosis**, is the closest anatomical neighbour and the option that most rewards care. It occupies the same sinus, and it too gives proptosis and chemosis. It differs in three ways: it \"usually resul[ts] from **infection** such as sinusitis, orbital or preseptal cellulitis or otitis\", it is \"Unilateral or often **BILATERAL**\", and it comes with severe headache, malaise, nausea and vomiting and a mortality of 20 % treated, up to 100 % untreated (`L10) orbit` slide 32). Clotted blood does not transmit a pulse; flowing arterial blood does.\n\n**How the two fistula types differ**, since the stem specifies the traumatic one (`L10) orbit` slides 34–35): a **direct** CCF is high-flow, post-traumatic, and gives the loud triad above with marked epibulbar dilatation, raised IOP from elevated episcleral venous pressure, and in severe cases anterior segment ischaemia. An **indirect** (dural) fistula is low-flow and spontaneous, with gradual redness, exaggerated ocular pulsation on applanation, and **'corkscrew' epibulbar vessels** — subtle enough that \"the condition may be overlooked\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Pulsating exophthalmos after head trauma is a direct carotid-cavernous fistula — the classic triad is pulsatile proptosis, chemosis and a bruit abolished by ipsilateral carotid compression.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.23 (Ch.3 Q3)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"Proptosis can be assessed by which of the following devices?",
    options:["Slit lamp biomicroscope","Hertel exophthalmometer","Ophthalmoscope","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The Hertel exophthalmometer is the instrument, and it is named in both sources.** The book defines proptosis as anterior displacement of the globe \"(measured by a **ruler or by Hertel exophthalmometer**)\" and specifies what is being measured: \"the distance between the **tip of the cornea** and the **plane of the lateral orbital margin**\", normally 12–22 mm (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.57). `L10) orbit` slide 8 gives the same instrument — printed \"Hertel exophthalmoeter\" [sic] — alongside the cruder method, \"Visualization from above\", and the threshold that readings over 20 mm indicate proptosis with an inter-eye difference of 2–3 mm being suspicious regardless of the absolute value.\n\nThe device works by resting on both lateral orbital rims at once and reading each corneal apex against a scale through angled mirrors, which is why the *difference* between the two eyes is as informative as either number.\n\n**A, the slit lamp biomicroscope**, examines the anterior segment under magnification — lids, conjunctiva, cornea, anterior chamber, lens. It cannot measure how far forward the globe sits, because the patient's head is fixed in the same frame as the instrument and there is no reference to the orbital rim.\n\n**C, the ophthalmoscope**, looks *inside* the eye. It is relevant to orbital disease — `L10) orbit` slide 13 catalogues the fundus changes that orbital disease causes: disc swelling, optic atrophy with choroidal folds, opticociliary shunt vessels and choroidal folds — but those are consequences of the orbital problem, not a measurement of proptosis.\n\n**D, none of the above**, is excluded once B is established.\n\nA point worth carrying: because a plain **ruler** is an accepted method in the book's own definition, the absence of an exophthalmometer is never a reason to leave proptosis unquantified. Q14 of this chapter tests exactly that — \"It may be measured by a ruler\" is printed there as a TRUE statement.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Proptosis is quantified with a Hertel exophthalmometer (or a ruler) as the distance from the corneal tip to the plane of the lateral orbital margin.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.23 (Ch.3 Q4)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"A 15-year-old boy received a fist blow during a quarrel with one of his companions. CT was ordered and it revealed a fracture in orbit bones. Which of the following is the most likely site that fractured?",
    options:["Medial orbital wall","Lateral orbital wall","Roof of the orbit","Floor of the orbit"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A fist to the eye is the textbook mechanism of a blow-out fracture, and the blow-out fracture breaks the floor.** The book is explicit on both halves. Mechanism: \"a sudden increase in the orbital pressure from an impacting object that is **greater in diameter than the orbital aperture (fist, tennis ball)** causing the eye ball to be displaced\". Site: \"The fracture **most frequently involves the floor of the orbit** along the thin bone covering the infraorbital canal. Sometimes, **the medial orbital wall** may also be fractured\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58, the floor highlighted in the book's own yellow). `L25) Trauma I` slide 20 gives the same history — \"History of trauma (FIST, TENNIS BALL)\".\n\nThe physics matters more than the memorised answer. An object **wider than the orbital rim** cannot enter the orbit; it loads the rim and the globe together, the orbital contents are driven backwards, pressure rises inside a rigid box, and the box gives way where it is thinnest. The rim itself — a thick ring of frontal, zygomatic and maxillary bone built to take exactly this blow — stays intact, which is why a blow-out fracture is defined by an intact rim with a broken wall.\n\n| Option | Bone | Why not the answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Medial wall** | Lamina papyracea of the ethmoid | Genuinely thin, and the book's own \"sometimes\" second site. It is the runner-up, not the most likely, and it far more often accompanies a floor fracture than occurs alone |\n| **Lateral wall** | Zygomatic and greater wing of sphenoid | The **thickest** and most protected wall; it takes direct high-energy trauma to break it |\n| **Roof** | Orbital plate of frontal | Breaks with severe craniofacial injury, not a punch, and it raises intracranial questions |\n| **Floor** | Maxilla over the infraorbital canal | Thin, unsupported over the maxillary sinus, and the site the book names |\n\n**A, the medial orbital wall**, is the option that deserves the care. It is thin bone — thin enough that orbital cellulitis spreads across it from the ethmoid, and that the subperiosteal abscess of orbital cellulitis is \"most frequently located along the **MEDIAL** orbital wall\" (`L10) orbit` slide 27). But the course material ranks the two for fracture and puts the floor first; \"most likely\" is what the stem asks.\n\n**The floor's thinness has a named consequence** the chapter returns to repeatedly: it is thin *along the infraorbital canal*, so the fracture line runs through the nerve. That is Q1 of this chapter, and it is why numbness of the cheek and upper teeth is a fracture sign rather than an incidental finding.\n\n⚠️ `L10) orbit`, the module's dedicated orbit deck, teaches **none of this** — its own NOT-COVERED register records that blow-out fracture mechanism, entrapment and orbital floor versus medial wall are absent, leaving only passing mentions on slides 5 and 10. The teaching lives in `L25) Trauma I` slides 19–24 and in `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.58–59.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A fist or ball wider than the orbital aperture raises orbital pressure and blows out the thinnest wall — the orbital floor over the infraorbital canal, with the medial wall a distant second.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.24 (Ch.3 Q5)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"For the 15-year-old boy in the previous case, who received a fist blow and whose CT revealed an orbital fracture, which of the following findings may be noticed following the trauma?",
    options:["Early proptosis","Late enophthalmos","Both of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A blow-out fracture pushes the eye forward first and lets it sink later, so both are true and C is the key.** The book prints the sequence in one sentence: \"Patients suffer from **early proptosis** due to oedema, emphysema, and haematoma but **later can develop enophthalmos**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58, clinical picture item 2).\n\n**The two signs are opposites in direction and consecutive in time, which is why the question works.** They have different mechanisms:\n\n| Phase | Sign | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Early** (hours to days) | **Proptosis** | Extra volume inside a fixed orbit — oedema, haematoma and surgical emphysema from air forced in through the broken sinus wall |\n| **Late** (weeks) | **Enophthalmos** | Lost volume — orbital fat and soft tissue herniate through the floor defect into the maxillary sinus, and the swelling that had been filling the orbit resolves |\n\nThe late sign is the one the sources treat as diagnostic of the fracture rather than of the injury. `L10) orbit` slide 5 lists the commonest cause of enophthalmos as \"fracture of orbital floor with **orbital fat prolapse**\"; `L25) Trauma I` slide 22 prints \"**ENOPHTHALMOS (SEVERE CASES)**\"; and the book gives \"Enophthalmos (severe cases)\" among the diagnostic features and \"large fractures causing marked enophthalmos\" as an indication for surgical repair (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59).\n\n**A, early proptosis alone**, and **B, late enophthalmos alone**, are each individually correct statements, and each is wrong here only because the other is also correct. This is the shape the chapter uses to test whether the sequence has been learned as a sequence rather than as a single sign.\n\n**D, none of the above**, is excluded by either.\n\n**The clinical consequence of the sequence is a trap for the examiner as well as the patient**: a fracture assessed in the first days may show a *forward* eye and be mistaken for having no floor defect at all. Q1 of this chapter is built on the same asymmetry — its patient presents with the **late** picture, enophthalmos plus infraorbital anaesthesia.\n\n**BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED.** The bank prints only \"In the previous case\". The antecedent was read, not assumed: it is **Q5** of this chapter, the 15-year-old boy who took a fist blow with a CT-proven orbital fracture, and the stem above restates him by mechanism and imaging. Q5's own answer — *which wall* fractured — is deliberately not restated, because it is Q5's key.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Blow-out fracture gives early proptosis from oedema, emphysema and haematoma, then late enophthalmos as swelling resolves and orbital fat herniates into the maxillary sinus.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.24 (Ch.3 Q6)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"For the 15-year-old boy in the previous case, who received a fist blow and whose CT revealed an orbital fracture, which of the following is the best line of management that is used in most of the cases?",
    options:["Surgical intervention","Conservative management and avoidance of nose blowing","Both of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Most blow-out fractures are managed without an operation, and the single instruction that matters most is: do not blow your nose.** The book's management list has three items in this order (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59):\n\n1. \"Patients should be advised to **avoid blowing the nose**.\"\n2. \"Prophylactic antibiotics\"\n3. \"**Surgical repair** will be required in case of: muscle entrapment causing diplopia, **or** large fractures causing marked enophthalmos\"\n\nSurgery is conditional — it appears with the words \"in case of\" — while the nose-blowing advice and the antibiotics are unconditional. `L25) Trauma I` slide 24 prints the same three, and widens the first: \"The patient is instructed to **REFRAIN FROM BLOWING THE NOSE** and, preferably coughing or sneezing\".\n\n**Why nose blowing is dangerous, and why it is not merely cosmetic advice.** The floor of the orbit is the roof of the maxillary sinus, and the fracture has opened a hole between the two. The book states the risk directly: \"**Infection can spread from the nasal sinuses to the orbit with nose blowing**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58, clinical picture item 1). Blowing the nose raises sinus pressure and drives air — and whatever the air is carrying — through the defect into the orbit. The immediate result is orbital surgical emphysema; the feared one is orbital cellulitis, with everything that follows it (Q2 and Q15 of this chapter). `L25) Trauma I` treats emphysema as the presenting sign of the fracture, giving it its own slide (slide 18) immediately before the blow-out slides.\n\n**A, surgical intervention**, is the answer to **Q8 of this chapter**, not to this one — the bank prints the two side by side with identical option lists so that the qualifying clause has to be noticed. Surgery is for the minority: entrapment with diplopia, or a large fracture with marked enophthalmos.\n\n**C, both of the above**, would make every patient a surgical patient, which inverts the book's \"in case of\".\n\n**D, none of the above**, is excluded once B is established, and would leave the sinus-to-orbit route unguarded.\n\n**The one part of the answer the option text omits** is antibiotic cover, which both sources give unconditionally — the book's item 2, flagged as a tutorial point (\"Prophylactic antibiotics (systemic Abs.. tutorial)\"), and `L25) Trauma I` slide 24's \"Systemic antibiotics\". A complete conservative plan is therefore: no nose blowing, systemic antibiotics, and review — because the indication for surgery may declare itself only as the oedema settles.\n\n**BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED, AND IT IS TWO LINKS DEEP.** The bank prints \"In the previous case\", but the literally previous item, Q6, is itself a back-reference and contains no patient. The antecedent was read: it is **Q5**, the 15-year-old boy with the fist blow and the CT-proven orbital fracture, restated above by mechanism and imaging only.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Most blow-out fractures are managed conservatively — avoid nose blowing (which drives sinus air and infection into the orbit) plus systemic antibiotics; surgery is reserved for entrapment or marked enophthalmos.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.24 (Ch.3 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"For the same 15-year-old boy who received a fist blow and whose CT revealed an orbital fracture, which of the following is the best line of management if the patient had limitation of ocular motility with diplopia?",
    options:["Surgical intervention","Conservative management and avoidance of nose blowing","Both of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Limited motility with diplopia after a floor fracture means a muscle is caught in the fracture, and a trapped muscle is released surgically.** The book's indication is printed as a condition: \"**Surgical repair** will be required in case of: **muscle entrapment causing diplopia** or large fractures causing marked enophthalmos\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59). The stem supplies the first condition exactly.\n\n**What is trapped, and why it limits movement.** The book names the tissue: \"**Mechanical entrapment** within the fracture of the **inferior oblique or inferior rectus muscles**, or adjacent connective tissue and fat\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59, the muscles highlighted in the book's own yellow; `L25) Trauma I` slide 21 is identical). Both are elevator-antagonising structures running along the floor, so incarcerating either tethers the globe downward.\n\n**The signature is diplopia in BOTH directions**, which both sources state and which is the finding that distinguishes entrapment from bruising: \"Diplopia occurs in **both upgaze and downgaze**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59; `L25) Trauma I` slide 21). A tethered muscle blocks elevation and, being incarcerated, also fails to relax normally on depression. `L10) orbit` slide 10 files the same phenomenon under ophthalmoplegia as \"**Tethering of muscles or tissue after orbital wall fracture**\".\n\n**B, conservative management**, is the answer to **Q7**, the immediately preceding question, and the two are printed with identical options so that the added clause must be read. It is not merely suboptimal here — the book's other named cause of post-fracture diplopia is \"**Haemorrhage and oedema** in the orbit with restriction to movement of the globe\", of which it says \"Ocular motility usually **improves** as the haemorrhage and oedema resolve\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59). That is the diplopia that deserves waiting. Entrapped tissue does not resolve, because nothing about it is swelling.\n\n**C, both of the above**, is the option that is nearly defensible and still wrong. A surgical patient does of course also avoid nose blowing and receive antibiotics. But the question asks for the **best line of management** for a specific complication, and pairing the definitive treatment with the treatment it replaces reads as indecision. The bank keys the intervention.\n\n**D, none of the above**, would leave a mechanically tethered muscle in a bone defect.\n\n**How the entrapment is confirmed before operating**: coronal CT. The book's shaded panel says \"**CT scan** of the orbit (coronal section) is important to diagnose floor fractures and tissue (inferior oblique muscle) entrapment\", and its tutorial box names what to look for — fracture of the floor, \"**Tissue entrapment within the fracture (Tear drop sign)**\", and surgical emphysema (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59; `L25) Trauma I` slide 23). That is Q11 of this chapter.\n\n*(The urgency of release — the paediatric white-eyed blow-out that needs operating within hours, and the conventional two-week window for other repairs — is not taken from the course material: `L25) Trauma I` records both as absent from its own slides, and the book gives no timing at all.)*\n\n**BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED, THREE LINKS DEEP.** \"The previous case\" cannot be Q7 or Q6 — both are back-references carrying no patient of their own. The antecedent is **Q5**, the 15-year-old boy with the fist blow and CT-proven orbital fracture, restated above.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Diplopia with limited motility after a floor fracture means inferior rectus or inferior oblique entrapment — diplopia in both upgaze and downgaze — and it is an indication for surgical repair, not observation.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.24 (Ch.3 Q8)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"A 38-year-old male patient presented with unilateral progressively-expanding exophthalmos following head trauma. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Grave's disease","Retrobulbar hemorrhage","Carotid-cavernous fistula","Cavernous sinus thrombosis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**An eye that is being pushed steadily further forward after trauma is being pushed by an expanding volume of blood.** The book's aetiology of exophthalmos gives retrobulbar haemorrhage exactly this profile: \"**Orbital/ Retrobulbar haemorrhage** — **Acute onset** — History of trauma (or surgery) — **Ophthalmic emergency due to markedly raised IOP with risk of CRAO**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58, item 4). `L10) orbit` slide 6 gives the mechanism generically: proptosis occurs when \"the globe [is] being pushed forward (**retrobulbar hge**, tumor etc)\".\n\n**This stem and Q3's are the same sentence with one word changed, and that word is the diagnosis.** Q3 says *pulsating*; this says *progressively-expanding*. Same age, same sex, same mechanism, same four options in the same order — and the keys are different.\n\n| Word in the stem | What it means physically | Diagnosis |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pulsating** | Arterial pressure transmitted into the orbital veins — an arteriovenous shunt (`L10) orbit` slide 12) | Carotid-cavernous fistula (Q3) |\n| **Progressively-expanding** | A growing volume inside a closed bony box | Retrobulbar haemorrhage (this question) |\n\n**C, carotid-cavernous fistula**, is therefore the deliberate near-miss, and it is excluded by the absence of a pulse, not by the presence of trauma — trauma causes 75 % of direct fistulae (`L10) orbit` slide 33). A CCF also tends to declare itself \"days or weeks after head injury\" (slide 34) rather than expanding steadily from the moment of impact.\n\n**A, Graves' disease**, has no traumatic route at all. It is autoimmune, driven by IgG antibodies against the TSH receptor, usually bilateral or bilateral-asymmetrical, and it develops over months with lid retraction, restrictive myopathy and the systemic picture of thyrotoxicosis (`L10) orbit` slides 15–20).\n\n**D, cavernous sinus thrombosis**, needs infection, not injury — \"usually resulting from infection such as sinusitis, orbital or preseptal cellulitis or otitis\" — and it is \"Unilateral or **often BILATERAL**\" with severe headache, malaise, nausea and vomiting (`L10) orbit` slide 32). The stem gives neither fever nor bilaterality.\n\n**Why this diagnosis is the emergency of the four to recognise at the bedside.** The orbit is a rigid cone closed anteriorly by the septum and the lids. Blood accumulating behind the globe has nowhere to go: intraorbital pressure rises, the eye is tented forward, the lids tighten, and perfusion of the optic nerve head and retina fails. That is what the book means by \"risk of **CRAO**\" — the retina tolerates arterial occlusion for a very short time, so the sight-saving intervention is decompression of the orbit, not imaging. *(The specific procedure — lateral canthotomy and inferior cantholysis — is not taken from the course material; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.58 defers the topic entirely, \"refer to ocular trauma chapter\".)* The corpus's `opqb-t3-132` describes the same syndrome from its signs: severe pain, proptosis, tight eyelids, decreased vision, elevated IOP.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Steadily expanding proptosis straight after trauma is retrobulbar haemorrhage — an acute compartment syndrome of the orbit that raises IOP and threatens CRAO; a pulsatile proptosis instead means a carotid-cavernous fistula.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.24 (Ch.3 Q9), answer line printed on p.25' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"Normal distance between corneal tip and plane of lateral orbital margin is around ... mm?",
    options:["8-10","12-22","20-25","15-25"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**12–22 mm, and the stem is a near-quotation of the book's own definition.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.57 defines exophthalmos as anterior displacement of the globe measured by ruler or Hertel exophthalmometer, then states: \"The measurement represents the **distance between the tip of the cornea and the plane of the lateral orbital margin**. Normal values range from **12 mm to 22 mm**.\"\n\nThe range is wide because it is a population range, not a diagnostic threshold. Deep-set orbits and prominent ones are both normal, which is why the *inter-eye difference* carries more weight than either absolute reading.\n\n⚠️ **THE TWO SOURCES DIVERGE HERE, AND THE KEY IS NOT AFFECTED.** `L10) orbit` slide 8 does not give a normal range at all; it gives a cut-off in the other direction — \"**Readings greater than 20 mm are indicative of proptosis** and a difference of **2–3 mm or more between the two eyes is suspicious** regardless of the [absolute value]\". Taken literally the two overlap awkwardly: the book calls 21 mm normal and the deck calls it proptotic. Both are the course's own material and neither is corrected here. The bank is quoting the book, and the book's range is option B. Note also that no distractor sits inside the overlap in a way that could be argued — **A (8–10)** is far below any normal orbit, **C (20–25)** is proptotic by both sources at its upper end and starts at the deck's own cut-off, and **D (15–25)** has the right width but the wrong floor and a frankly abnormal ceiling.\n\n**Why the landmark is the lateral orbital margin.** It is the one bony point of the orbital rim that is superficial, palpable and unaffected by lid position or soft-tissue swelling, so the exophthalmometer can rest on it reproducibly in a swollen orbit. The instrument reads both eyes against the same pair of rims in one placement, which is what makes the 2–3 mm asymmetry rule usable.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The exophthalmometric normal is 12-22 mm from corneal tip to the plane of the lateral orbital margin; an inter-eye difference of 2-3 mm is suspicious whatever the absolute value.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.25 (Ch.3 Q10)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"Orbital floor fractures are best assessed with which of the following diagnostic imaging modalities?",
    options:["MRI","X ray","Ultrasound","CT coronal section","CT axial section"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Not just CT — CT in the CORONAL plane, and the bank prints both CT options to force that distinction.** The book's shaded panel says it in one line: \"**CT scan** of the orbit (**coronal section**) is important to diagnose floor fractures and tissue (inferior oblique muscle) entrapment\", and its tutorial box lists what the coronal images show — fracture of the orbital floor ± another wall, \"**Tissue entrapment within the fracture (Tear drop sign)**\", and ± surgical emphysema (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59). `L25) Trauma I` slide 23 prints the identical three-item list under the heading \"CT scan of the orbit (**CORONAL SECTION**)\".\n\n**The geometry is the whole answer.** The orbital floor is a roughly horizontal plate. An **axial** slice runs parallel to it, so a floor defect is cut edge-on and can be missed entirely or smeared across several slices; herniated fat sitting below the floor is indistinguishable from the sinus contents. A **coronal** slice cuts the floor at right angles, so the defect appears as a break in a line and the prolapsed tissue hangs into the maxillary sinus as the **tear-drop** the tutorial box names.\n\n| Option | Why it is not best here |\n|---|---|\n| **MRI** | `L10) orbit` slide 14 assigns MRI to \"orbital apex lesions, orbital tumours and imaging orbital inflammatory disease\" — soft tissue. Cortical bone gives almost no MR signal, so a fracture line is what MRI shows worst |\n| **X ray** | The deck is blunt: \"Plain X-rays are **little used** except for the initial diagnosis of traumatic bony injury\" (`L10) orbit` slide 14). A screening role at most; it cannot show entrapment |\n| **Ultrasound** | Listed among orbital investigations (`L10) orbit` slide 14; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.57) but useless across bone, which reflects the beam, and it cannot see into the sinus |\n| **CT axial** | Right modality, wrong plane — see the geometry above |\n\n**Why CT is the modality even though the clinical question is about a muscle.** `L10) orbit` slide 14 gives the division of labour: \"**Computed tomography (CT) is useful for depicting bony structures** and the location and size of space-occupying lesions.\" A blow-out fracture is a bony injury whose complication happens to be soft-tissue, and CT resolves both — bone windows for the defect, soft-tissue windows for the herniated fat and muscle.\n\n**Note the bank's spelling**: option B is printed \"X ray\", unhyphenated, and is reproduced as printed. Q26 of this chapter hyphenates the same word.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Coronal CT is the investigation for a suspected orbital floor fracture — it cuts the floor at right angles and shows the defect, the tear-drop of entrapped tissue and any surgical emphysema.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.25 (Ch.3 Q11)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"All of the following are among the causes of diplopia observed in a patient with blow-out fracture except?",
    options:["Hemorrhage and edema","Mechanical entrapment","Traumatic nerve palsy","Direct injury to extraocular muscles"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Three of these four options are the book's own numbered list, in the book's own order, and the fourth was invented for this question.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.59 prints under \"Diplopia due to:\"\n\n1. \"**Haemorrhage and oedema** in the orbit with restriction to movement of the globe. Ocular motility usually improves as the haemorrhage and oedema resolve.\"\n2. \"**Mechanical entrapment** within the fracture of the inferior oblique or inferior rectus muscles, or adjacent connective tissue and fat. Diplopia occurs in both upgaze and downgaze.\"\n3. \"**Direct injury** to an extraocular muscle\"\n\nOptions A, B and D map onto items 1, 2 and 3. **C, traumatic nerve palsy**, is not on the list, and it is the key.\n\n**Why the omission is anatomical rather than arbitrary.** All three printed causes are **mechanical or inflammatory events inside the orbit** — blood, trapped tissue, torn muscle. A cranial nerve palsy would require injury to the third, fourth or sixth nerve, and those run in the posterior orbit, the superior orbital fissure and the cavernous sinus, far from the floor that a blow-out fracture breaks. The blow-out mechanism — a fist wider than the orbital aperture, raising pressure and rupturing the thinnest wall (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58) — is an anterior event.\n\n**The distractor is nevertheless clinically real**, which is what makes it a good one. `L10) orbit` slide 10 lists \"**Oular** [sic] motor nerve involvement (cavernous sinus, orbital fissures or posterior orbit)\" among the causes of ophthalmoplegia in general — but under carotid-cavernous fistula, Tolosa–Hunt syndrome and malignant lacrimal gland tumours, not under fracture. A cranial nerve palsy after head trauma belongs to the head injury, not to the orbital floor.\n\n**The three real causes separate on tempo and direction, and that separation drives management:**\n\n| Cause | Diplopia pattern | What happens next |\n|---|---|---|\n| Haemorrhage and oedema | Restriction in whatever direction the swelling loads | \"Usually **improves** as the haemorrhage and oedema resolve\" — observe |\n| Mechanical entrapment | **Both upgaze AND downgaze** | Does not resolve; surgical release (Q8 of this chapter) |\n| Direct muscle injury | Depends on which muscle is torn | Assessed once swelling settles |\n\nThat is why the same chapter can key *conservative* for the general case (Q7) and *surgical* for the case with limited motility (Q8): the first two causes look alike in the first days, and only the entrapped one is still there in the third week.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Diplopia after a blow-out fracture comes from haemorrhage and oedema, mechanical entrapment of inferior oblique or inferior rectus, or direct muscle injury — not from a traumatic cranial nerve palsy.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.25 (Ch.3 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"All of the following are among the causes of proptosis except?",
    options:["Orbital cellulitis","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Retrobulbar hemorrhage","Tumor metastases"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Read option D as written — it says *metastases*, not *tumours* — and the key stops looking wrong.** The book files the two on opposite sides of its own divide (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58):\n\n| Aetiology of **exophthalmos** (proptosis) | Aetiology of **enophthalmos** |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Graves' disease — commonest in adults | 1. Orbital fat atrophy (post-traumatic or old age) |\n| 2. Orbital cellulitis — commonest in children | 2. **Cicatrising tumour metastasis** |\n| 3. Orbital **tumours** — gradual onset, usually unilateral | 3. \"Blow-out\" fracture |\n| 4. Orbital/retrobulbar haemorrhage — acute, post-traumatic | |\n| 5. Cavernous sinus thrombosis | |\n| 6. Carotid-cavernous fistula (pulsating exophthalmos) | |\n\nOptions A, B and C are items 2, 5 and 4 of the left-hand column. Option D belongs to the right-hand one.\n\n**The distinction the bank is testing is between a mass and a scar.** A primary orbital tumour adds volume to a closed box and pushes the globe forward — that is item 3, and it is a cause of proptosis, which **Q25 of this chapter states explicitly** as a true characteristic of orbital tumours. A **cicatrising** metastasis does the opposite: certain metastatic deposits, scirrhous breast carcinoma being the classic, provoke dense fibrosis that **contracts** the orbital contents and pulls the eye backwards. Same disease category, opposite sign, because the tissue reaction differs. *(The named primary is not taken from the course material — the book says only \"Cicatrising tumour metastasis\" and names no tumour; `L10) orbit` mentions metastasis once, in Table 4.1's differential of an acutely inflamed orbit.)*\n\nQ13 and Q25 are therefore two halves of one fact, printed twelve questions apart and keyed in opposite directions. Getting one right and the other wrong is the diagnostic failure the pair is designed to catch.\n\n**A, orbital cellulitis**, proptoses by inflammatory volume behind the septum, and the book's comparison table lists proptosis as **present** in orbital cellulitis and **absent** in preseptal (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.61).\n\n**B, cavernous sinus thrombosis**, obstructs the venous outflow of the orbit. `L10) orbit` slide 32 lists \"Unilateral or often **BILATERAL** proptosis, chemosis, congestion of the facial, conjunctival and retinal veins\" — the orbit engorges because nothing is draining.\n\n**C, retrobulbar haemorrhage**, is the fastest of them: an acute volume of blood behind the globe, an ophthalmic emergency with markedly raised IOP and a risk of CRAO (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58). It is the answer to Q9 of this chapter.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Orbital cellulitis, cavernous sinus thrombosis and retrobulbar haemorrhage all proptose the eye; cicatrising tumour metastasis is filed the other way, as a cause of enophthalmos.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.25 (Ch.3 Q13)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding exophthalmos except?",
    options:["It may be caused by orbital fat atrophy due to old age","Pseudo-exophthalmos must be excluded first","It may be measured by a ruler","CT/MRI orbit is usually required"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Orbital fat atrophy empties the orbit, so the eye sinks rather than protrudes — it is the book's first cause of ENOPHTHALMOS, printed on the same page as the exophthalmos list.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.58: \"**Aetiology:** 1. **Orbital fat atrophy ( post- traumatic or in old age)** 2. Cicatrising tumour metastasis 3. \\\"Blow-out\\\" fracture\". `L10) orbit` slide 5 gives the same picture from the other end — enophthalmos is the globe \"being retracted backwards\", commonly from an orbital floor fracture with fat prolapse, radiotherapy or a sclerosing lesion.\n\nThe reasoning is volumetric and it works in both directions. The orbit is a fixed bony cone with a fixed capacity. **Add** volume — inflammation, blood, a mass, engorged veins — and the globe is the only thing that can move, so it moves forward. **Lose** volume — fat atrophy with age, fat prolapsed through a fractured floor, fibrosis pulling the contents back — and the globe settles backwards. Option A describes a loss, so it cannot be a cause of a protrusion.\n\n**The remaining three are all printed truths about exophthalmos**, and each is worth its own line:\n\n**B, pseudo-exophthalmos must be excluded first.** The book prints this as a standing instruction rather than an afterthought: \"**Note: Pseudo-exophthalmos: (must be ruled out)**\" with its three causes — lid retraction, contralateral ptosis, and the large globe of high myopia (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.57). The point is that an eye can *look* proptosed without being displaced at all, and the exophthalmometer settles it in seconds. Q22 of this chapter tests the causes.\n\n**C, it may be measured by a ruler.** Also verbatim: proptosis is \"measured by a **ruler** or by Hertel exophthalmometer\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.57). The ruler is the low-tech version of the same measurement — corneal tip to the plane of the lateral orbital margin — and the book gives it equal standing. Q4 of this chapter asks for the instrument.\n\n**D, CT/MRI orbit is usually required.** The book's general investigations for exophthalmos are \"1. **CT/MRI of the head and/or orbits** 2. Ultrasound of the orbits 3. Thyroid function tests\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.57), CT/MRI first. `L10) orbit` slide 14 splits the roles: CT for bony structures and the location and size of space-occupying lesions, MRI for orbital apex lesions, tumours and inflammatory disease.\n\n**The question's structure is worth noticing**: options B, C and D are the three steps of an actual clinical workflow — rule out the mimic, measure it, image it — and option A is a fact about the opposite condition dropped into that sequence. Chapter 3 uses this device twice more (Q13 and Q25), each time by moving something across the exophthalmos/enophthalmos line.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Exophthalmos requires excluding pseudo-exophthalmos, is measured by ruler or exophthalmometer and usually imaged by CT/MRI; orbital fat atrophy with age is a cause of enophthalmos, not of protrusion.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.25 (Ch.3 Q14), option D and answer line printed on p.26' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"Which of the following is the percentage of people who develop loss of vision due to orbital cellulitis?",
    options:["5-10%","7-11%","10-15%","15-20%"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**7–11 %, and the figure is printed as the first bullet of the book's complications list.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.60: under \"**Complications**\" — \"**7-11% will suffer significant loss of vision.**\" The three distractors are neighbouring round numbers with no source behind them.\n\nThe figure is worth attaching to the rest of that page rather than memorising alone, because it is the number that justifies how aggressively the condition is treated. The same page prints, beside the complications, a boxed \"**Death**\" panel: orbital cellulitis \"is life-threatening if untreated\", with a \"**Mortality of 17-20% without antibiotic use**\", and the heading above the whole section reads \"It is an OCULAR as well as a **MEDICAL EMERGENCY**\".\n\n| Number on `ophthalmology.pdf` p.60 | What it counts |\n|---|---|\n| **7–11 %** | Significant **loss of vision** |\n| 17–20 % | **Mortality** without antibiotics |\n\nThe rest of the complications list explains where the vision goes: \"Optic nerve inflammation (possible loss of vision)\", cavernous sinus thrombosis, meningitis, brain abscess. `L10) orbit` slide 27 expands the ocular half — \"optic neuropathy, exposure keratopathy, raised IOP, endophthalmitis and **occlusion of the central retinal artery or vein**\" — and adds the subperiosteal abscess, \"most frequently located along the **MEDIAL** orbital wall\", which is the collection that has to be drained.\n\n⚠️ **NO PERCENTAGE FOR THIS APPEARS IN THE LECTURES.** Both `L10) orbit` and the book were read end to end for this entry: the deck gives the clinical picture, the complications and the treatment of orbital cellulitis over slides 26–29 and prints **no figure at all** for visual loss. Its only orbital percentages are elsewhere — cavernous sinus thrombosis mortality (20 % treated, up to 100 % untreated, slide 32), direct carotid-cavernous fistulae caused by trauma (75 %, slide 33), and capillary haemangioma resolution (30 % by age 3, about 75 % by age 7, slide 40). The book is the sole source for 7–11 %, which is consistent with the user's ruling that the book is this module's main reference.\n\n**The practical reading**: roughly one patient in ten loses meaningful vision, and the finding that identifies them early is optic nerve involvement — reduced acuity, impaired colour vision, and above all an RAPD, which the book's comparison table marks as a **poor prognostic sign** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.61). That is the finding in Q2 of this chapter, and it is why monitoring of optic nerve function is a listed part of treatment (`L10) orbit` slide 29).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"7-11 % of orbital cellulitis patients suffer significant visual loss, and mortality without antibiotics is 17-20 % — the numbers behind calling it a medical as well as ocular emergency.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.26 (Ch.3 Q15)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common cause of exophthalmos in children?",
    options:["Orbital cellulitis","Grave's disease","Orbital tumors","Cavernous sinus thrombosis"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**In children the commonest cause is orbital cellulitis; in adults it is Graves' disease. The book highlights both words in yellow on the same page** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58): item 1, Graves' disease, \"Most common cause in **adults**\"; item 2, orbital cellulitis, \"Most Common Cause in **children**\". `L10) orbit` slide 26 says the same of the disease itself — bacterial orbital cellulitis is \"**More common in children**\".\n\n**Why childhood shifts the answer.** Orbital cellulitis arises \"typically from the paranasal (especially **ETHMOID**) sinuses\" (`L10) orbit` slide 26); the book gives \"sinus/ facial/ tooth infections or trauma\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60). Children get frequent sinus infections, and the lamina papyracea between the ethmoid air cells and the orbit is thin — so the commonest infection of childhood sits one sheet of bone away from the orbit. Graves' disease, meanwhile, is a disease of adult life, so the adult answer is simply unavailable in this age group.\n\n**B, Graves' disease**, is the answer to **Q20 of this chapter**, which prints this identical option list in the identical order and changes only *children* to *adults*. The two questions are a pair and neither is redundant.\n\n**C, orbital tumors**, is where the reading has to be precise, because two different \"commonest\" facts collide. The commonest *cause of proptosis* in children is orbital cellulitis. The commonest *orbital tumour* in childhood is **capillary haemangioma** — \"THE MOST COMMON TUMOUR OF THE ORBIT AND PERIORBITAL AREA IN CHILDHOOD\" (`L10) orbit` slide 40) — and the commonest *primary orbital malignancy* in children is **rhabdomyosarcoma** (`L10) orbit` slide 46; `L24) Ocular tumors` slide 78). Tumours as a class are not the leading cause of a proptosed child's eye; infection is.\n\n**D, cavernous sinus thrombosis**, is a *complication* of the correct answer rather than a competitor to it. The book lists it among the complications of orbital cellulitis (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60), and `L10) orbit` slide 32 gives its usual cause as \"infection such as sinusitis, orbital or preseptal cellulitis or otitis\". It is far rarer than the condition that produces it.\n\n**The presentation that matters clinically** is the one that must not be mistaken for cellulitis: rhabdomyosarcoma causes \"RAPIDLY PROGRESSIVE UNILATERAL PROPTOSIS\" and \"**may mimic an inflammatory condition such as orbital cellulitis**\" (`L10) orbit` slide 46). A proptosed child who is afebrile, or who fails to respond to intravenous antibiotics, needs imaging for a mass rather than a longer course.\n\n⚠️ **CORPUS NOTE FOR THE SWEEP, NOT A FOLD**: `opqb-t3-135` (Grade Gain) prints the same stem in list form — \"Most common cause of exophthalmos in children:\" — and keys the same answer, but its options are Lymphoma / Orbital cellulitis / Cavernous sinus thrombosis / Retinoblastoma, sharing only two of four with this one.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Orbital cellulitis is the commonest cause of proptosis in children (ethmoid sinusitis across the lamina papyracea), as Graves' disease is in adults — and neither is the same question as the commonest orbital tumour.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.26 (Ch.3 Q16)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"All of the following are among the features of orbital cellulitis except?",
    options:["Absent chemosis","Decreased ocular motility","Elevated ESR","Fever"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Chemosis in orbital cellulitis is not absent — the book calls it MARKED — so the option that says it is absent is the exception.** The question is doubly inverted: the stem asks for what is false, and the false option is itself a denial. Read it as \"chemosis is absent in orbital cellulitis\", which is a statement about **preseptal** cellulitis.\n\nThe book's comparison table decides all four rows (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.61):\n\n| Clinical feature | Preseptal | Orbital |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Chemosis** | **Absent** | **Marked** |\n| **Ocular mobility** | Normal | **Decreased** |\n| **ESR** | Elevated or normal | **Elevated** |\n| **Fever** | May be present | **Present** |\n\n`L10) orbit` slide 26 lists the same features as prose for bacterial orbital cellulitis: \"Malaise and **Pyrexia**, often marked\", \"periocular and conjunctival (**chemosis**) oedema, conjunctival injection\", \"**Painful ophthalmoplegia**\", diplopia, RAPD and proptosis. Slide 24 states the preseptal contrast: \"proptosis and chemosis are **ABSENT** and VA, pupillary reactions and ocular motility are **UNIMPAIRED**\".\n\n**Why chemosis marks the septal line.** Chemosis is oedema of the bulbar conjunctiva, and the bulbar conjunctiva is continuous with the tissue planes *behind* the orbital septum. Infection anterior to the septum swells the lid and stops there; infection posterior to it raises pressure in the orbit and the fluid tracks forward under the conjunctiva. The same anatomy explains the row beneath it: only a posterior infection can reach the extraocular muscles, so only a posterior infection restricts movement.\n\n**B, decreased ocular motility**, is true and is one of the two findings (with RAPD) that carry the most diagnostic weight — `L10) orbit` calls it painful ophthalmoplegia, and pain **on movement** is itself a separate row of the book's table (absent preseptal, present orbital).\n\n**C, elevated ESR**, is true of orbital cellulitis, and the table shows why it is a weak discriminator rather than a strong one: preseptal cellulitis reads \"Elevated **or normal**\". A raised ESR supports infection without localising it. The white cell count behaves the same way — moderate leucocytosis preseptally, marked in orbital disease — and `L10) orbit` slide 29 lists the white cell count among the investigations.\n\n**D, fever**, is true and again present in both, differing only in reliability: \"May be present\" preseptally against \"Present\" in orbital cellulitis. `L10) orbit` calls the pyrexia \"often marked\".\n\n**The discriminating findings, ranked**: chemosis, proptosis, pain on eye movement, decreased mobility, diminished vision and RAPD are all **absent or normal** preseptally and **present** in orbital cellulitis. The blood tests and the fever are shared. Q24 and Q28 of this chapter ask the same table from the preseptal side.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Chemosis is marked in orbital cellulitis and absent in preseptal — as are proptosis, restricted painful motility and RAPD; fever and a raised ESR occur in both and discriminate poorly.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.26 (Ch.3 Q17)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"What is the most common causative organism of preseptal cellulitis in adults?",
    options:["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","E-Coli","H. Influenza","A&B"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**The book pairs a staphylococcus with a streptococcus for adults, so the combined option is the key.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.60, under the aetiology of preseptal cellulitis: \"Suspect **H. Influenzae in children**; **S. Aureus or Streptococcus in adults**.\" Two organisms are named for adults, and options A and B are the two — hence **E, A&B**.\n\n`L10) orbit` slide 24 gives the same pair for preseptal cellulitis and adds the routes of entry: local skin trauma such as laceration or insect bites; spread from a periocular infection — acute hordeolum, dacryocystitis, conjunctivitis, sinusitis; or haematogenous spread from the upper respiratory tract or middle ear. All three routes are skin-and-adnexa routes, which is exactly why the skin commensals lead.\n\n⚠️ **A SPECIES DIVERGENCE BETWEEN BANK AND SOURCE, RECORDED, KEY UNCHANGED.** The bank's option B names **Streptococcus pneumoniae**. `L10) orbit` slide 24 names \"Staphylococcus aureus and **Streptococcus PYOGENES**\" for preseptal cellulitis, and the book names only \"Streptococcus\" with no species. *S. pneumoniae* does appear in the module — but on `L10) orbit` slide 26, for **orbital** cellulitis, where the list is \"Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes and Haemophilus influenzae\". So the bank has taken the streptococcus from the wrong side of the septum. The key is still **E**, because the question asks whether *two* organisms are involved rather than one, and both sources answer yes; the species mismatch does not change which letter is correct. Per the standing rule, the printed key is not disputed and the discrepancy is recorded here. **What the safer answer would be:** *S. aureus* and *S. pyogenes* (a beta-haemolytic Group A streptococcus) for preseptal disease in adults.\n\n**C, E-Coli** [sic], is a gut organism with no route to the periorbital skin, and it is named nowhere in either source for orbital or preseptal infection.\n\n**D, H. Influenza** [sic — *Haemophilus influenzae*], is the answer to **Q19 of this chapter**, the children's version, printed immediately after this one with the same options minus the fifth. Its historical dominance in paediatric preseptal cellulitis is what makes the paediatric answer differ from the adult one at all.\n\n**Why the organism decides the antibiotic.** The book's treatment of preseptal cellulitis is \"Systemic antibiotics e.g., **(amoxicillin-clavulanic acid.)**\" — a choice that covers staphylococci, streptococci and *Haemophilus* together — plus the safety rule that \"If severe or in a child aged one year or less, treat as orbital cellulitis\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60), which escalates to admission and intravenous therapy.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Adult preseptal cellulitis is a skin-flora infection — Staphylococcus aureus and a streptococcus together — covered by amoxicillin-clavulanate; Haemophilus influenzae is the paediatric organism.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.26 (Ch.3 Q18)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"What is the most common causative organism of preseptal cellulitis in children?",
    options:["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","E-Coli","H. Influenza"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Haemophilus influenzae is the organism the book tells you to suspect in a child.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.60: \"Suspect **H. Influenzae in children**; S. Aureus or Streptococcus in adults.\" This question and Q18 are printed consecutively with the same option list — Q18 carries a fifth option, \"A&B\" — so the same letter D means the same organism in both, and only the age band moves the key.\n\n`L10) orbit` slide 26 names *Haemophilus influenzae* among the common causative organisms of **orbital** cellulitis alongside *Streptococcus pneumoniae*, *Staphylococcus aureus* and *Streptococcus pyogenes*, and records that orbital cellulitis is \"More common in **children**\". The paediatric orbit and periorbit are thus the *Haemophilus* territory on both sides of the septum.\n\n| Option | Where the sources place it |\n|---|---|\n| **Staphylococcus aureus** | Adult preseptal cellulitis (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60; `L10) orbit` slide 24) and orbital cellulitis (slide 26) — the answer to Q18, paired with a streptococcus |\n| **Streptococcus pneumoniae** | Orbital cellulitis (`L10) orbit` slide 26). Not the paediatric preseptal organism |\n| **E-Coli** [sic] | Named nowhere in either source for any orbital or periorbital infection |\n| **H. Influenza** [sic] | \"Suspect H. Influenzae **in children**\" — the key |\n\n**Why the age band changes the organism.** Preseptal cellulitis in adults follows skin breaches — trauma, insect bites, hordeolum, dacryocystitis (`L10) orbit` slide 24) — so it grows the skin flora. In young children a large share arrives haematogenously or from the upper airway, where *Haemophilus influenzae* type b colonises, and it seeds the periorbital tissue from the bloodstream.\n\n*(That Hib conjugate vaccination has made this organism much less common in immunised populations is not taken from the course material: neither `L10) orbit` nor `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.3 mentions immunisation status for cellulitis at all. The one immunisation the deck does raise is unrelated — \"Ascertainment of **tetanus** immunization status in cases of trauma\", `L10) orbit` slide 29.)*\n\n**The safety rule attached to the paediatric answer** is the part that matters at the bedside, and the book prints it immediately after the antibiotic: \"If severe or in a **child aged one year or less**, treat as **orbital** cellulitis\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60) — that is, admit, culture, image and give intravenous antibiotics, because the septum is a poor barrier at that age and the clinical distinction is unreliable.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Haemophilus influenzae is the organism to suspect in paediatric preseptal cellulitis; in a child of one year or less, or any severe case, treat as orbital cellulitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.26 (Ch.3 Q19), options C-D and answer line printed on p.27' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common cause of exophthalmos in adults?",
    options:["Orbital cellulitis","Grave's disease","Orbital tumors","Cavernous sinus thrombosis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Thyroid eye disease is the commonest cause of a protruding eye in an adult, and both sources say so in as many words.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.58 lists Graves' disease first among the causes of exophthalmos and highlights the word: \"Unilateral or bilateral — **Most common cause in adults**\". `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` states it independently: thyroid eye disease \"is also the **most common cause of unilateral or bilateral protrusion of the globes, or exophthalmos**\".\n\nThis question and **Q16** are a matched pair — identical options in identical order, *adults* against *children* — so the two answers must be held apart rather than merged into one \"commonest cause of proptosis\".\n\n**Unilateral disease does not exclude it.** The commonest reason a candidate rejects this option is that Graves' disease is systemic and therefore \"should\" be bilateral. Thyroid eye disease is frequently **asymmetrical** — `L10) orbit` slide 17 illustrates \"moderate bilateral **asymmetrical** lid retraction\" — and the book's own entry reads \"Unilateral **or** bilateral\". Because the condition is so much commoner than every orbital tumour combined, even its unilateral presentations outnumber the alternatives.\n\n**A, orbital cellulitis**, is the children's answer (Q16). In adults it is much less common, and it announces itself with fever, pain on eye movement and a raised white count rather than with the slow lid signs of thyroid disease.\n\n**C, orbital tumors**, is the option that needs the ranking kept straight, because a different \"commonest\" belongs to it. The commonest orbital **tumour** in adults is **cavernous haemangioma** — \"It is THE MOST COMMON ORBITAL TUMOUR IN ADULTS\", slowly progressive unilateral proptosis, well encapsulated, treated by surgical excision (`L10) orbit` slide 41; `L24) Ocular tumors` slide 77). The commonest **malignant** orbital tumour in older adults is **lymphoma**, typically involving the lacrimal gland or orbital fat with a characteristic \"molded\" shape (`L24) Ocular tumors` slide 78). Neither makes tumours as a class the commonest *cause of proptosis*.\n\n**D, cavernous sinus thrombosis**, is rare and acute — infective in origin, often bilateral, with severe headache, malaise, nausea and vomiting, cranial nerves III–VI affected, and a mortality of 20 % even treated (`L10) orbit` slide 32). It is a differential for the acutely inflamed orbit, not a common cause of proptosis.\n\n**What to do with the answer clinically.** Ask about the systemic picture — weight loss despite good appetite, heat intolerance, palpitations, tremor (`L10) orbit` slide 15) — and look for the lid signs: Dalrymple (lid retraction), Kocher (severe retraction), von Graefe (lid lag on downgaze) (`L10) orbit` slide 17). Then check what must not be missed: `L10) orbit` slide 20 says to test visual acuity, colour desaturation, RAPD and visual fields **regularly** for compressive optic neuropathy, because that is the finding that turns lubricants and smoking cessation into intravenous methylprednisolone and orbital decompression (slides 22–23).\n\n⚠️ **CORPUS NOTE FOR THE SWEEP**: two Grade Gain entries are close but neither is a stem match — `opqb-t3-113` (\"Most common cause of adult **unilateral** proptosis\", options Thyroid orbitopathy / Metastasis / Lymphoma / Meningioma) and `opqb-t3-117` (\"The commonest cause of **unilateral** exophthalmos is\", which shares two of four options with this one). Both insert *unilateral*, which this stem does not.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Thyroid eye disease is the commonest cause of adult exophthalmos, unilateral or bilateral; the commonest orbital tumour in adults is a different question, answered by cavernous haemangioma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.27 (Ch.3 Q20)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"A 26-year-old male patient developed anaesthesia and paraesthesia in the region of lower lid, cheek and side of nose following a severe head injury. Which of the following is the structure that fractured and caused such manifestations?",
    options:["Fallopian canal","Supraorbital canal","Infraorbital canal","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The sensory territory names the nerve, and the nerve names the canal it runs in.** The book ties the three together in one sentence: \"**Infraorbital nerve anaesthesia:** Involving the **lower lid, cheek, side of the nose**, upper lip, upper teeth and gums **if the fracture involves the infraorbital canal**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59; `L25) Trauma I` slide 22 is identical). The stem gives the first three items of that list.\n\nThe canal is where it is because of what lies beneath it. The book explains that a blow-out fracture \"most frequently involves the floor of the orbit **along the thin bone covering the infraorbital canal**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58) — the nerve runs forward in a groove and then a canal in the orbital floor, which is also the roof of the maxillary sinus, and emerges at the infraorbital foramen. That strip is the thinnest part of the floor, so the fracture line and the nerve share a course. `L10) orbit` slide 2 labels the **infraorbital groove** and the **infraorbital foramen** on its bones-of-the-orbit figure, and `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy` labels the groove, canal and nerve together.\n\n**⚠️ Two of the four options are structures the ophthalmology material never teaches, and that was measured rather than assumed.** A case-insensitive grep for \"fallopian\" across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters returns **zero hits**; \"supraorbital\" returns only four, all of them the **supraorbital NOTCH** or the supraorbital artery on anatomy figures (`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy` and `L10) orbit` slide 2) — **no \"supraorbital canal\" exists in any cached source**.\n\n**A, the Fallopian canal**, is a real structure incorrectly placed. It is the facial nerve's canal within the petrous temporal bone, and it is nowhere near the orbit; fracturing it gives facial palsy, and possibly hearing loss or taste disturbance, but never numbness of the cheek. *(That the Fallopian canal carries the facial nerve is standard anatomy and is not taken from the course material — see the grep above.)*\n\n**B, the supraorbital canal**, is a near-miss on the name only. The corresponding real structure is the supraorbital **notch or foramen** in the frontal bone, and the nerve leaving it is the supraorbital branch of the **ophthalmic** division (V1), supplying the forehead, the upper lid and the scalp — the territory *above* the palpebral fissure, i.e. everything the stem does not describe. The infraorbital nerve is a branch of the **maxillary** division (V2). V1 and V2 divide the face at the fissure, and the stem's findings are all below it.\n\n**D, none of the above**, is excluded once C is established.\n\n⚠️ **THIS IS THE CHAPTER'S SECOND PRINTING OF THE SAME FACT.** `opmcq-c3-1` asks for the **nerve** and keys the infraorbital nerve, with a longer symptom list (adding upper lip, upper teeth and gums) and a different option set, and it adds enophthalmos to the picture. The bank writes the same fact in American spelling there (\"anesthesia\") and British here (\"anaesthesia\", \"paraesthesia\"), six questions apart.\n\n**One point of care in the stem**: it says \"severe head injury\", not a fist blow, so the mechanism need not be the classic blow-out. Any midfacial fracture crossing the orbital floor or the maxilla can involve the canal.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Numbness over the lower lid, cheek and side of nose localises to the infraorbital canal in the orbital floor — V2 territory — whereas the supraorbital notch carries V1 to the forehead and the Fallopian canal carries the facial nerve.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.27 (Ch.3 Q21)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"All of the following are among the causes of pseudo-exophthalmos except?",
    options:["Lid retraction","Contralateral ptosis","Orbital cellulitis","High axial myopia"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Orbital cellulitis proptoses the eye for real, so it cannot be a cause of a false proptosis.** The other three are the book's list, verbatim and complete: \"**Note: Pseudo-exophthalmos: (must be ruled out)** It is due to: **Lid retraction / Contralateral ptosis / The large globe in high myopia**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.57).\n\n**Pseudo-exophthalmos means the globe is where it should be and only looks displaced.** The distinction is settled by measurement, not by inspection — corneal tip to the plane of the lateral orbital margin, 12–22 mm, by ruler or Hertel exophthalmometer (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.57; Q4 and Q10 of this chapter). Each of the three real causes fools the eye by a different route:\n\n| Cause | Why the eye looks proptosed |\n|---|---|\n| **Lid retraction** | The lids sit further apart, so more sclera shows above and below the limbus. The globe has not moved — the *aperture* has widened |\n| **Contralateral ptosis** | The comparison is corrupted rather than the eye. A drooping lid on the other side makes the normal side look staring |\n| **High axial myopia** | The globe is genuinely **larger** — a long eye in a normal orbit fills more of it, so the cornea sits further forward. Enlargement, not displacement |\n\n**C, orbital cellulitis**, is a true cause of proptosis: the book's comparison table marks proptosis **present** in orbital cellulitis and **absent** in preseptal (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.61), and `L10) orbit` slide 26 lists proptosis among its signs. Inflammatory volume behind the septum pushes the globe forward, and an exophthalmometer confirms it.\n\n**Lid retraction deserves a second glance**, because it is the option most likely to be mis-selected as the exception. It is both a cause of pseudo-proptosis *and* a real sign of thyroid eye disease, where it coexists with genuine proptosis — `L10) orbit` slide 17 catalogues the eponymous grades (Dalrymple, Kocher, von Graefe) and slide 18 shows \"bilateral proptosis **with** lid retraction\". The two mechanisms add up in the same patient, which is exactly why the book insists pseudo-exophthalmos be ruled out **before** the exophthalmos is worked up (Q14 of this chapter tests that instruction).\n\n⚠️ **A HEADING ERROR IN THE DECK, RECORDED AND NOT DISPUTED.** `L10) orbit` slide 9 prints this list — lid retraction, high myopia, buphthalmos, facial asymmetry, contralateral enophthalmos — under the heading \"**Pseudoptosis**\". Every item listed is a cause of pseudo-**proptosis**, not of a falsely drooping lid, and the slide sits between the proptosis-measurement slide and the ophthalmoplegia slide, exactly where a pseudo-proptosis slide belongs. The book's heading is the correct one, and the bank follows the book. No key is affected either way. The deck's list is also **wider** than the book's: it adds **buphthalmos** (an enlarged globe from congenital glaucoma, the same enlargement mechanism as high myopia), **facial asymmetry** (a shallower orbit on one side), and **contralateral enophthalmos** (the mirror image of contralateral ptosis — the comparison eye has sunk).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Pseudo-exophthalmos is lid retraction, contralateral ptosis or a large globe (high myopia, buphthalmos) — the eye only looks displaced; orbital cellulitis produces measurable, genuine proptosis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.27 (Ch.3 Q22)' },

  /* opmcq-c3-23 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-24.
     A within-chapter reprint of `opmcq-c3-5` (book p.24): the SAME four options in
     the SAME order, the SAME key, and the same fist-blow-orbital-fracture scenario.
     The stems differ only in the patient age (15 vs 26) and in Q5 naming the CT.
     Q5 is held rather than Q23 because Q5 IS THE ANTECEDENT of Q6 and Q7, which
     both open "For the 15-year-old boy in the previous case" -- folding it would
     strand two back-references.
     ⚠️ Q27 is the same fact a THIRD time and is NOT folded: bare recall, no patient,
     options reordered AND renamed. A vignette and a recall question are met
     differently by a reader. Held, with the relationship recorded in its own text. */
  { id:'opmcq-c3-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"Which of the following is found in preseptal cellulitis?",
    options:["Chemosis","Proptosis","Pain on eye movement","Normal ocular motility"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three of these four are printed as ABSENT in preseptal cellulitis; the fourth is printed as NORMAL, and normal is what is \"found\".** The book's comparison table answers all four rows at once (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.61):\n\n| Clinical feature | **Preseptal** | Orbital |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chemosis | **Absent** | Marked |\n| Proptosis | **Absent** | Present |\n| Pain on eye movement | **Absent** | Present |\n| **Ocular mobility** | **Normal** | Decreased |\n\n`L10) orbit` slide 24 states the same in one sentence: in preseptal cellulitis, \"in contrast to orbital cellulitis, **proptosis and chemosis are ABSENT** and VA, pupillary reactions and **ocular motility are UNIMPAIRED**.\"\n\n**The anatomy is the answer, and it is a single sheet of tissue.** The orbital septum is the fibrous curtain running from the orbital rim into the lids. Infection anterior to it — the book's definition of preseptal cellulitis is \"Infection of soft tissue **anterior** to the orbital septum\" — is confined to the eyelid and periorbital skin. It cannot reach the extraocular muscles, so the eye moves normally and painlessly; it cannot add volume to the orbital cone, so the globe stays where it is; and it cannot track under the bulbar conjunctiva, so there is no chemosis. Orbital cellulitis is the same infection on the other side of that curtain — \"Inflammation of orbital contents **posterior** to the orbital septum\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60) — and every sign follows from the change of compartment.\n\n**A, chemosis**, is the marker of posterior disease and is the answer to Q17 of this chapter, where \"Absent chemosis\" is the false statement about orbital cellulitis.\n\n**B, proptosis**, is the sign that most reliably separates the two at a glance. A preseptal patient may have a lid so swollen the eye cannot be seen — which is why the lid must be opened. The book's own figure for preseptal cellulitis shows exactly that manoeuvre: a child with the fissure closed by swelling, then the lid lifted to reveal \"a quiet white globe underneath\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60, Fig 3-4).\n\n**C, pain on eye movement**, requires inflamed muscles or an inflamed orbit for them to move in. `L10) orbit` slide 26 calls the orbital version \"**Painful ophthalmoplegia**\"; preseptal disease has tender lids, but the eye itself does not hurt to move.\n\n**Do not read \"normal ocular motility\" as reassurance to be acted on once.** The clinical difficulty is that the two conditions can look alike early, which is why the book adds an escalation rule to the preseptal treatment: \"If severe or in a **child aged one year or less**, treat as orbital cellulitis\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60). Motility is re-checked, not checked.\n\n⚠️ **CORPUS NOTE — A MIRROR, NOT A REPRINT**: `opqb-t3-134` (Grade Gain) asks \"In preseptal cellulitis, which of the following is **absent**?\" and keys **Proptosis**, from this same table with the polarity reversed and a different option set (Proptosis / Fever / Eyelid edema / Leukocytosis). Flagged for the sweep. This chapter's own **Q28** asks the table a third way and keys \"Normal pupil reaction\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Preseptal cellulitis leaves the globe untouched — motility normal and painless, no proptosis, no chemosis — because the infection lies anterior to the orbital septum.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.27 (Ch.3 Q24), options B-D and answer line printed on p.28' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"All of the following are among the characteristics of orbital tumors except?",
    options:["Gradual onset","Usually unilateral","Among the causes of exophthalmos","Among the causes of enophthalmos"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Orbital tumours push the eye OUT, and the book files them under exophthalmos — so \"among the causes of enophthalmos\" is the exception.** Options A, B and C are the book's own three bullets, verbatim, printed as aetiology item 3 of exophthalmos (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58):\n\n> 3) **Orbital tumours** — Gradual onset — Usually, unilateral (but can be bilateral in lymphoma) — With or without associated systemic disease e.g., neurofibromatosis\n\n**This question and Q13 are one fact printed from both ends.** Q13 asks for the cause of proptosis that is *not* one, and keys \"Tumor **metastases**\", because the book's enophthalmos list contains \"**Cicatrising** tumour metastasis\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58). The distinction is between a mass, which occupies space and displaces the globe forward, and a fibrosing deposit, which contracts the orbital contents and draws the globe back. Both are neoplastic; only one is a tumour *characteristic* in the sense this question means.\n\n**A, gradual onset**, is the tempo that separates a tumour from everything else on the same aetiology list — Graves' disease evolves over months but with lid signs and a systemic picture; orbital cellulitis and retrobulbar haemorrhage are acute. `L10) orbit` describes the two commonest benign tumours in exactly those terms: cavernous haemangioma gives \"Slowly progressive unilateral proptosis\" (slide 41), and pleomorphic lacrimal gland adenoma gives \"PAINLESS SLOWLY PROGRESSIVE proptosis or swelling in the superolateral eyelid, usually of **MORE THAN A YEAR'S DURATION**\" (slide 42).\n\n⚠️ **The gradual rule has named exceptions the book does not print**, and they are the dangerous ones. Lacrimal gland **carcinoma** gives \"proptosis of **RAPID ONSET**\" with bony erosion on CT and adenoid cystic carcinoma at 50 % of cases (`L10) orbit` slide 43), and **rhabdomyosarcoma** — the commonest primary orbital malignancy of children — gives \"**RAPIDLY PROGRESSIVE UNILATERAL PROPTOSIS** ... [that] may mimic an inflammatory condition such as orbital cellulitis\" (`L10) orbit` slide 46). A fast-growing orbital mass is not excluded by being fast.\n\n**B, usually unilateral**, is true with the book's own parenthesis attached: \"but can be **bilateral in lymphoma**\". `L24) Ocular tumors` slide 78 names lymphoma \"the most common malignant orbital tumour in older adults\", frequently involving the lacrimal gland or orbital fat, with a characteristic \"**molded**\" shape around the globe.\n\n**C, among the causes of exophthalmos**, is where the book puts the whole category.\n\n⚠️ **WHERE THE TUMOUR BIOLOGY IN THIS ENTRY COMES FROM.** The book's entire orbital-tumour section is the three bullets quoted above — no named tumour, no imaging appearance, no management. `L10) orbit` slides 38–46 and `L24) Ocular tumors` slides 77–78 are the sources for everything beyond them: dermoid cyst (a choristoma, superotemporal, painless), capillary haemangioma (commonest orbital tumour of childhood, 30 % resolve by 3 years and 75 % by 7, treated for amblyopia with propranolol), cavernous haemangioma (commonest orbital tumour in adults), optic nerve glioma (children, median 6.5 years, 30 % with neurofibromatosis type 1, fusiform enlargement on CT), optic nerve sheath meningioma (visual loss **before** proptosis; triad of progressive visual loss, optic atrophy and opticociliary shunt vessels; commoner in NF2), and adenoid cystic carcinoma (severe pain from perineural invasion).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Orbital tumours are of gradual onset, usually unilateral (bilateral in lymphoma) and a cause of exophthalmos; only cicatrising tumour metastasis is filed under enophthalmos.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.28 (Ch.3 Q25)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"A 50-year-old woman with a history of hypothyroidism presents with new-onset unilateral proptosis and diplopia in upgaze. Despite being clinically hypothyroid, her eye findings strongly suggest orbital involvement. What is the most appropriate imaging study to confirm the diagnosis?",
    options:["CT brain","MRI orbit","Ultrasound globe","X-ray skull"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The suspected lesion is swollen extraocular muscle, which is soft tissue, and the imaging that resolves orbital soft tissue is MRI of the orbit.** `L10) orbit` slide 14 divides the modalities explicitly: CT \"is useful for depicting **bony structures** and the location and size of space-occupying lesions\", while \"**Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can demonstrate orbital apex lesions, orbital tumours and imaging orbital inflammatory disease**\". Thyroid eye disease is an orbital inflammatory disease, and the deck's own illustration of it is an MRI — slide 21 is an \"Axial MRI, arrows on the **enlarged extraocular muscle bellies**\". The book's general investigation of exophthalmos is \"CT/MRI of the head and/or orbits\" first, ultrasound second, thyroid function tests third (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.57).\n\n**Why the stem is thyroid eye disease and not something else.** Unilateral proptosis with **diplopia in upgaze** in a middle-aged woman is restrictive myopathy — `L10) orbit` lists \"**Restrictive myopathy** (e.g. thyroid eye disease)\" first among the causes of defective ocular motility (slide 10) and gives it its own clinical heading under TED, \"Double vision and often discomfort in some positions of gaze\" (slide 19). The muscle that restricts elevation is the inferior rectus, a tethered depressor. *(That the inferior rectus is the muscle most often involved in thyroid ophthalmopathy is not taken from the course material — `L10) orbit` never ranks the muscles, and its own register records no such list. The corpus entry `opqb-t3-137` keys inferior rectus from the other bank.)*\n\n⚠️ **THE STEM RESTS ON A FACT NEITHER SOURCE STATES, AND HERE IS HOW THAT WAS MEASURED.** Thyroid eye disease is repeatedly presented as a disease of Graves **hyperthyroidism**: \"Graves disease, the most common form of **hyperthyroidism**, is an autoimmune disorder in which IgG antibodies bind to thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH) receptors ... and stimulate secretion of thyroid hormones\", with weight loss despite good appetite, heat intolerance and palpitations (`L10) orbit` slide 15); `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` calls it \"an autoantibody-mediated enlargement of the extraocular muscles, orbital fat, and lacrimal gland\". A case-insensitive grep for \"euthyroid|hypothyroid\" across all 29 cached ophthalmology lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters returns **exactly one hit** — `ch05-eyelids.txt` line 843 — and that hit was opened: it is \"Hypothyroidism\" listed as risk factor 2 for **xanthelasma**, unrelated to the orbit. **So the module never teaches that thyroid eye disease occurs in hypothyroid or euthyroid patients.** Filling the gap: the orbital disease is driven by autoantibodies against the TSH receptor shared by orbital fibroblasts, and it runs on its own timetable — patients may be hyperthyroid, euthyroid or hypothyroid (including after treatment, or in Hashimoto thyroiditis) when the eye signs appear, so a normal or low thyroid state never excludes the diagnosis. *(Not taken from the course material.)* That is precisely what the stem's \"Despite being clinically hypothyroid\" is testing.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **CT brain** | Wrong region and wrong window. A brain study does not image the orbital apex or the muscle bellies at useful resolution; CT's orbital role is bone and space-occupying lesions (`L10) orbit` slide 14). CT *orbit* would be a reasonable alternative — CT **brain** is not |\n| **Ultrasound globe** | Ultrasound of the **orbit** is a listed investigation (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.57; `L10) orbit` slide 14), but *globe* ultrasound images the eye itself — vitreous, retina, intraocular tumours — not the retrobulbar muscles, and it cannot see the apex |\n| **X-ray skull** | \"Plain X-rays are **little used** except for the initial diagnosis of traumatic bony injury\" (`L10) orbit` slide 14). No soft-tissue information at all |\n\n**What the MRI is looking for**: fusiform enlargement of the muscle bellies with sparing of the tendon insertions, most often inferior and medial rectus, and crowding at the orbital apex. The apex is the finding that changes management, because it is where the optic nerve is compressed — which is why `L10) orbit` slide 20 requires visual acuity, colour desaturation, RAPD and visual fields to be tested **regularly**, and why compressive optic neuropathy is treated with intravenous methylprednisolone and orbital wall decompression (slide 23) rather than with the lubricants and smoking cessation of mild disease (slide 22).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Suspected thyroid eye disease is imaged by MRI of the orbit, which resolves the enlarged extraocular muscle bellies and the apex; the orbitopathy can occur in hypothyroid or euthyroid patients, so thyroid status never excludes it.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.28 (Ch.3 Q26)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"In a blow-out orbital fracture caused by blunt trauma, which orbital wall is most commonly affected?",
    options:["Roof","Medial wall","Floor","Lateral wall"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The floor, and the book says so in the sentence that defines the injury:** \"The fracture **most frequently involves the floor of the orbit** along the thin bone covering the infraorbital canal. Sometimes, the medial orbital wall may also be fractured. Those thin walls of the orbit separate the orbital cavity from the surrounding nasal sinuses\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58, the floor highlighted in the book's own yellow).\n\nThis is the chapter's third printing of the fact and the only one without a patient — `opmcq-c3-5` builds a fist-blow vignette around it, and the bank printed that vignette TWICE (p.24, and again as Q23 on p.27 with identical options, identical key, and only the patient age changed) -- which is why the two vignette printings are held as ONE entry and this bare-recall printing is not. **⚠️ It is also the printing whose options are reordered and renamed** (bare \"Roof / Medial wall / Floor / Lateral wall\" instead of \"Medial orbital wall / Lateral orbital wall / Roof of the orbit / Floor of the orbit\"), so the correct letter here is **C** where the other two are D. The order above is the bank's, unaltered.\n\n**Why a blunt blow finds the floor.** A blow-out fracture is defined by its mechanism: \"a sudden increase in the orbital pressure from an impacting object that is **greater in diameter than the orbital aperture (fist, tennis ball)**\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.58; `L25) Trauma I` slide 20). The object is too wide to enter the orbit, so it cannot strike a wall directly — it compresses the rim and the globe, pressure rises inside a closed cone, and the pressure finds the weakest surface. The rim is built to take the blow and does; the floor, thin over the infraorbital canal and spanning the empty maxillary sinus with nothing behind it, is not.\n\n| Wall | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Roof** | Orbital plate of the frontal bone, and above it the anterior cranial fossa. It breaks with severe craniofacial trauma, not a punch |\n| **Medial wall** | Lamina papyracea — genuinely thin, and the book's own \"sometimes\" second site, usually in company with a floor fracture rather than alone. Buttressed behind by the ethmoid air-cell septa *(the buttressing point is standard anatomy, not taken from the course material)* |\n| **Lateral wall** | Zygomatic bone and greater wing of sphenoid, the thickest and most protected wall |\n\n**What follows from the floor being the answer** — the rest of the chapter, in effect. The infraorbital canal runs in that floor, so its nerve is anaesthetised (Q1, Q21). The inferior rectus and inferior oblique lie on it, so they can be incarcerated, giving diplopia in **both upgaze and downgaze** (Q8, Q12). The maxillary sinus lies beneath it, so nose blowing drives air and infection upward into the orbit (Q7). The lost volume drains into that sinus, so early proptosis becomes late enophthalmos (Q6, Q14). And a horizontal plate is best cut at right angles, so the investigation is **coronal** CT (Q11).\n\n⚠️ `L10) orbit`, the module's dedicated orbit deck, records in its own NOT-COVERED register that blow-out fracture mechanism, entrapment management and \"orbital floor vs medial wall\" are absent from it. The teaching is in `L25) Trauma I` slides 19–24 and `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.58–59.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The orbital floor is the wall blown out by blunt trauma — thin over the infraorbital canal and unsupported above the maxillary sinus — with the medial wall the book's occasional second site.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.28 (Ch.3 Q27)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c3-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of preseptal cellulitis?",
    options:["Proptosis","Painful eye movements","Normal pupil reaction","Decreased vision"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A normal pupil reaction is the feature that belongs to preseptal cellulitis; the other three are what happens when the infection is behind the septum.** The book's comparison table settles every option (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.61):\n\n| Clinical feature | **Preseptal** | Orbital |\n|---|---|---|\n| Proptosis | **Absent** | Present |\n| Pain on eye movement | **Absent** | Present |\n| **RAPD** | **Absent** | In severe cases (**poor prognostic sign**) |\n| Vision | **Normal** | Diminished ± diplopia |\n\n`L10) orbit` slide 24 states the same as a single contrast: in preseptal cellulitis, \"in contrast to orbital cellulitis, proptosis and chemosis are ABSENT and **VA, pupillary reactions and ocular motility are UNIMPAIRED**.\" A normal pupil is therefore not an incidental negative — it is one of the three findings the deck uses to define the condition.\n\n**Why the pupil is the most informative of the four.** The afferent limb of the light reflex is the optic nerve, which runs the length of the orbit **behind** the septum. Infection anterior to the septum has no access to it, so the pupils stay equal and briskly reactive however swollen the lid is. An RAPD means the nerve is being compressed or inflamed inside a rigid box, which is why the book marks it a **poor prognostic sign** and why `L10) orbit` slide 29 makes \"Monitoring of optic nerve function\" part of treatment. The corollary is a bedside rule: a hot swollen lid with a normal pupil, normal acuity and full painless movement can be treated as preseptal; any one of those failing moves the patient to admission and intravenous antibiotics.\n\n**A, proptosis**, is absent because the infection adds no volume to the orbital cone. The lid can be swollen shut without the globe moving — the book's own Fig 3-4 shows the lid being lifted to reveal a quiet white globe (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60).\n\n**B, painful eye movements**, requires inflamed muscles or an inflamed orbit for them to move in. `L10) orbit` slide 26 calls the orbital version \"**Painful ophthalmoplegia**\". Preseptal disease has a tender lid, not a painful eye.\n\n**D, decreased vision**, is an orbital sign — \"Diminished ± Diplopia\" in the table, and `L10) orbit` slide 26 adds that \"VA may be reduced and colour vision impaired, **raising the possibility of optic nerve compression**\".\n\n**The one caveat that keeps this from being a purely academic distinction**: the book's preseptal treatment carries an escalation rule — \"If severe or in a **child aged one year or less**, treat as orbital cellulitis\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.60) — because the septum is a poor barrier in infancy and because 7–11 % of orbital cellulitis patients lose significant vision (Q15 of this chapter).\n\n⚠️ **THE CHAPTER'S LAST QUESTION**, and its third pass at the same table: `opmcq-c3-17` asks it from the orbital side (keyed \"Absent chemosis\"), `opmcq-c3-24` asks which feature is *found* preseptally (keyed \"Normal ocular motility\"), and this one keys the adjacent row, \"Normal pupil reaction\". Different option sets, different keys — three questions, not one reprinted three times.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Preseptal cellulitis spares everything behind the septum — normal pupil reaction, normal vision, no proptosis, painless movement — and any of those failing means orbital cellulitis until proven otherwise.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.28 (Ch.3 Q28)' }
